 \keywords{linguistic steganography, large language models, rank transcoding, exact-copy replay, neural steganalysis}
 
 \begin{abstract}
-Cryptographic artifacts such as hashes, nonces and ciphertexts are readily recognizable in their usual textual forms. RankCloak conceals this syntax by mapping serialized artifacts to language-model token ranks and expressing them as generated cover text. Its bounded codecs and segmented generation provide a reproducible framework for studying recovery, efficiency and contextual acceptability. Across 480 deterministic artifacts from eight classes, three model families and 18 English prompts, all 6,480 primary trials recovered the original bytes under preserved token sequences and shared configurations. In a contextual evaluation with blinded model judges, 537 of 576 message pairs had complete ratings. Automated acceptance reached 90.3\% for RankCloak and 96.6\% for matched greedy ordinary output under a rubric allowing minor awkwardness. Human perception was not measured. Entropy gating reduced selected detection signals, with tradeoffs in payload rate and completion. Visible-text retokenization recovered 88 of 144 covers, while transformations and configuration mismatches exposed transport sensitivity. Steganalysis remained effective, and surface concealment does not imply undetectability or confidentiality. These results establish artifact specific concealment and configured recovery, with quantified efficiency, acceptability and transport tradeoffs that inform the design of language-model-based information hiding.
